00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:51.660
Do you know what’s in your water?

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.579
Do you know what’s buried deep in those
depths?

00:00:55.579 --> 00:01:01.340
Sure, lakes and ponds have an idyllic quality
to them, and you could even picture yourself

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:06.640
spending a nice fall day in a canoe, your
hand passing through waters that feel cool

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.570
and refreshing against your skin.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:15.210
But then, perhaps something else brushes up
against your hand too, a light touch you ignore

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:19.420
the first time, and then the second time as well.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:23.740
Even the third time, maybe you manage to keep
your composure, though the movement is more

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:27.420
insistent and squirming than the first few
contacts.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:32.250
And as it keeps happening, you realize you have
to see what it is.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.614
So you peek into the water, and there they
are.

00:01:35.614 --> 00:01:37.159
Worms.

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:38.250
Lots of them.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:43.509
And their bodies can tell us more about those
waters than you might even want to know.

00:01:57.409 --> 00:01:59.880
The worms themselves are not that scary, though,

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:04.960
from what we’ve learned, we imagine that
identifying them is going to be kind of a nightmare.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:10.210
These worms are members of the genus Nais,
and even though there are only roughly 30

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:15.360
Nais species, we’re not sure exactly which
one we’re looking at here.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.069
That ambiguity feels a little incongruent with the
worm that we see.

00:02:19.069 --> 00:02:22.930
This is not a particularly subtle creature
to look at.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.420
There are large squiggles and bright colors.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:30.090
But the specific feature that scientists use
to determine what species they’re looking at

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:33.280
is this.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.790
Those are tiny hairs, known better as chaetae.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.239
And as you can see, they’re quite small.

00:02:39.239 --> 00:02:43.970
And the number of features you could use to
describe them seems minimal.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:50.319
Yet scientists have to rely on these tiny
structures to try and apply a name tag to

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:52.100
the worms they’re studying.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:55.730
In the words of one paper, the differences
between these chaetae

00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:02.160
“are often subtle, inconsistent, and even
overlapping between nominal species”.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.159
Honestly, it sounds like a nightmare to me.

00:03:05.159 --> 00:03:10.650
James, our master of microscopes, usually
finds these worms in the ponds around him.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.120
They’re very common, and they’re also
very useful…

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:16.140
just in a kind of creepy way.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.110
Because these worms dance.

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:26.690
And not to insult them or anything, but their dances are 
kind of unsettling to watch.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:31.300
The worms are partially buried in the sediment,
but the part that we can see…

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:33.460
well, they’re hard to miss.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:37.959
They look like a hallucination you might conjure
up of The Rockettes performing choreography

00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:39.590
inspired by an Edvard Munch painting.

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:43.739
But the odd thing is that this dancing…

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:46.840
this is what makes the worms useful to James.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:51.110
If the worms are dancing like this, it means
that there’s a lot of oxygen dissolved in

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:52.110
the water.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:57.120
When the oxygen concentration starts to go
down though, the worms will start to emerge

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.190
further from the sediment.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:03.920
And as the levels keep going down, the worms
keep rising and rising in the water until

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.230
they’re right under the water line.

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:11.819
And as they rise, they stop dancing.

00:04:11.819 --> 00:04:18.109
So for James, who is often looking out for organisms
who might prefer a low-oxygen environment,

00:04:18.109 --> 00:04:24.840
the dancing of the worms are his own biological
oxygen meter, helping him gauge the conditions

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:28.400
and its effects on the creatures he’s looking
after.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:35.220
And while this is very neat, and very useful
for James, it is also a bit of a mystery because

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:39.160
we have no idea why they do this.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:44.160
We’ve looked through papers and tried to
find an explanation, but we only have our

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:45.160
guesses.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:50.660
James thinks their dance is most likely some
kind of way to filter the water around them

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:51.660
for food.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:56.580
But if any of you have an explanation, we
would love to hear it.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:01.880
This dancing isn’t the first time someone
has used worms from this genus to understand

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:03.070
the waters around them.

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:08.430
In fact, nais worms have been an important
part of a widespread scientific effort to

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:12.951
understand something far more horrific.

00:05:23.449 --> 00:05:31.180
Between 1995 and 1996, researchers ventured
to a lake near the village of Yanov in Ukraine

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:32.750
to find worms.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:38.009
The village had been evacuated nearly a decade
before, emptied of people and filled with

00:05:38.009 --> 00:05:44.110
radiation following the explosion of the Chernobyl
Nuclear Power Plant in 1986.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:49.720
But plenty of animals remained, and scientists
wanted to know what the effect of radiation

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:54.039
in Chernobyl had been on them and the ecosystem
around them.

00:05:54.039 --> 00:05:58.770
Some scientists looked at mice, others looked
at carp and catfish.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:01.479
These scientists chose worms.

00:06:01.479 --> 00:06:06.919
After all, they’re incredibly important
in aquatic ecosystems, growing into large

00:06:06.919 --> 00:06:13.020
populations that help transport and transform
organic matter throughout their watery world.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:18.620
So they gathered worms in the lake near Chernobyl,
including two species of Nais worms.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:22.890
And then they gathered the same species from
a lake 20 kilometers away.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:28.210
These lakes were similar in many ways: similar
temperatures, similar chemical compositions.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:32.220
The major difference, of course, was the radiation.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:36.400
The sediments near Chernobyl were loaded with
radioactive strontium—

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:39.960
around 55 times more than that in the distant
lake.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:44.470
And the worms themselves were getting dosed
with around 20 times more radiation in that

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:45.800
contaminated lake.

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:51.960
That radiation broke up bits of the worm’s
DNA, leaving fragments that were then patched

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:54.620
back up—
but not perfectly.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:58.910
The scientists could see the mistakes left
behind in the worm’s chromosomes.

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:01.900
It’s one thing to find a mistake or two
in the cells—

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:03.560
that can happen spontaneously.

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:09.099
But what the scientists found was more than
double the rate of that kind of mistake, leaving

00:07:09.099 --> 00:07:14.090
behind a build-up of aberrations embedded
genetically within the worm.

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:20.389
And the scientists also found something else,
a change of behavior in the worms.

00:07:20.389 --> 00:07:25.330
Nothing like the dance we saw earlier, but
something that’s unexpected for these worms:

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:26.330
sex.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:31.139
Nais worms can reproduce sexually, but they
tend not to.

00:07:31.139 --> 00:07:36.699
In fact, this is part of why they’re difficult
to study compared to worms in related genuses.

00:07:36.699 --> 00:07:40.620
Often, those worms are distinguished by the
shape of their genitals.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:46.960
But the Nais are so rarely engaged in sexual
reproduction that they don’t often have

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:52.699
what scientists delicately describe as a
“fully-developed sexual apparatus.”

00:07:52.699 --> 00:07:58.310
That lack of obvious genitalia makes it
difficult to identify them.

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:04.220
And yet in the contaminated lake, Nais worms
were more likely to pursue sexual reproduction

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:09.400
compared to their counterparts, a strategy
likely meant to help them introduce variations

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:18.389
in their genome that might allow them to adapt
to the new stresses of their environment, particularly that radiation.

00:08:18.389 --> 00:08:24.610
Decades later, perhaps that lake is filled
with the worms borne of those many couplings.

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:30.210
And maybe they’re there, dancing in the
sediment, while they bear the genetic legacy

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:35.430
of a catastrophic event.

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:40.490
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us

